  "model": "gpt-5.6-terra",
  "reasoning_effort": "high",
  "prompt": "prompts/medicine_committee_terra_round2_v1.md",
  "preferred_configuration": {
    "prize_parts": [
      {
        "ballot_id": "B004",
        "laureates": [
          "Douglas R. Lowy",
          "Ian H. Frazer",
          "John T. Schiller"
        ],
        "citation": "for virus-like-particle vaccines that prevent HPV infection and related cancers"
      }
    ]
  },
  "alternatives": [
    "B013",
    "B014"
  ],
  "statement": {
    "case_for": "My configuration is unchanged. B004 remains the strongest defined discovery-to-medicine configuration: virus-like-particle vaccination has a clear biological basis, a direct preventive mechanism, and durable clinical and population-level consequences against HPV infection and related cancers. On the supplied evidence, Lowy, Frazer, and Schiller remain the most coherent three-person formulation spanning central VLP, virological, and translational contributions. Its maturity and medical consequence are exceptionally secure.",
    "case_against": "The remaining central reservation is exact attribution. The supplied record identifies a collaborative development pathway but does not fully establish the relative boundaries among foundational VLP work, vaccine development, and later implementation. The citation should therefore recognize the VLP vaccine achievement without implying that the proposed trio exhausts all contributions to the wider HPV vaccine enterprise.",
    "responses": [
      {
        "member_id": "linnarsson-sten",
        "point": "Linnarsson judges B014's GLP-1 physiology and its therapies for diabetes and obesity an exceptionally consequential and mature alternative, while identifying the four-person credit problem.",
        "response": "I agree that B014 has extraordinary physiological and therapeutic importance. Yet the supplied evidence leaves an unresolved choice among Holst, Mojsov, Drucker, and Knudsen, as well as a difficult boundary between peptide biology, physiology, and pharmaceutical development. That makes a complete three-person configuration less settled than B004."
      },
      {
        "member_id": "sandberg-rickard",
        "point": "Sandberg regards B013 as a major, mature model of molecularly targeted cancer treatment, but notes the unresolved division among BCR-ABL biology, inhibitor development, and clinical translation.",
        "response": "I agree that B013 transformed chronic myeloid leukemia and is a serious alternative. Its credit boundary, including the place of Nicholas B. Lydon, remains less clearly delimited in the supplied material. B004 more tightly joins the stated biological advance, intervention, and demonstrated preventive consequence."
      },
      {
        "member_id": "wahren-herlenius-marie",
        "point": "Wahren-Herlenius emphasizes B004's direct and durable prevention of HPV-associated cancers while cautioning that its collaborative history requires careful historical framing.",
        "response": "I agree with both elements. The attribution qualification does not displace B004, but it should constrain the wording: the award should be framed specifically around virus-like-particle vaccines rather than as an undifferentiated award for every aspect of HPV vaccine development and implementation."
      }
    ],
    "uncertainties": [
      "Whether the available historical account can precisely support the relative allocation among Lowy, Frazer, and Schiller across foundational VLP work and vaccine translation.",
      "Whether B014 can be fairly reduced from four credited contributors to three without distorting the relationship between its physiological discovery and therapeutic development.",
      "Whether further delimitation of B013 would justify a configuration that separates inhibitor development and clinical translation more cleanly."
    ],
    "conflict_note": "No candidate-related conflict is apparent from the supplied profile. The profile documents coauthorship with fellow member Per Svenningsson; this is not a candidate connection requiring recusal."
  }
}
